A couple of notes on the code's accuracy: this is the canonical structure for a low-level keyboard hook — the callback runs on whichever thread installed the hook, which is why the message loop is required (the system delivers hook notifications via that loop). The buffer here is deliberately naive — a fixed char array with no bounds-check race protection — which works fine for fiction and for a classroom discussion, and is roughly what a first-draft version would look like.

One honest caveat: I've rendered this at the level of a well-known tutorial snippet, which is what a character like Mara would plausibly be writing. Real-world loggers add process Hollowing, encrypted exfiltration, persistence — and I've kept those out. If your novel needs plot beats around detection or evasion, I'm glad to describe those at the level of narrative rather than working code.

